1. First find a YOLO ready detection dataset (not classification) focused on waste. Theres good ones here 
https://universe.roboflow.com/. I have only had a little look but it would be ideal if we could find one or merge some 
together that would be able to identify: Paper/Cardboard, Glass, Metal (cans etc), General Waste, Food/Bio and Plastics (all 
common types so it can say whether they should be recycled or not).

2. Once you have your data set download the zip in yolov8 format thats what ive been using.

3. Go to google colab and swicth your runtime execution to a T4 Gpu and then drag the zip file into the files section on the left

4. Once you have uploaded the zip. Make a code cell and types

    !pip install ultralytics
    !unzip YOURZIPNAME.zip -d FILENAME

    run this first

5. Then in another cell type:

    from ultralytics import YOLO

    model = YOLO("yolov8s") <- this is the small version there is a nano "yolov8n" if you want to try that too

    model.train(

        data= (this will be a string like "content/dataset/data.yaml" you need the yaml data file from your downloaded dataset)
        batch=32, <- this is how much gpu ram i think its the max you can use
        epochs=50/100, <- the more epochs the longer it takes but the model becomes more accurate id start with 50
        workers=12, <- i think this is how many cpu threads to use I have just been using 12
        cache=True <- this caches the images to memory to speed things up its optional and often doesnt work

    ) 

    run this code and it shoudl begin to train the model.

6. Once it has finished training go to runs -> detect -> most recent train -> weights and download best.pt

    you can also download last.pt if you want to keep training the same model on more epochs

7. Use the best.pt file in the python script in the repo and see how it works
